Cg a67.375000,67.375000,24
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\fs48\b 2  Digital (FIR) Filters}}
.TXT 3 0 453 0 0
Cg a57.750000,57.750000,46
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\fs28 
______________________________________________}}
.TXT 4 12 358 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,603
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 To 
introduce the idea of filtering, we discuss its common application to 
noise smoothing. Consider a data sequence \{x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \} 
that is the sum of a signal \{s}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \} that varies 
little over a time duration L and a noise \{no}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \} 
that varies independently. Suppose that each value x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{
\cf2  is replaced by y}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2  which is the average of 
L contiguously-indexed values, including x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 . 
Recall that averaging L independent noise samples reduces their 
variance by 1/L. Therefore, we have smoothed the noise while changing 
the signal little. Such a filter is called a moving average. We 
consider this application in detail in Example 2.3.}}
.TXT 22 0 359 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,978
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 We 
note some important attributes of this filter. Notice that the 
operation of averaging is linear in that the signal and noise 
sequences are averaged separately. Of course, we are averaging a 
finite number of data values. Such a filter is said to have a finite 
impulse response, denoted FIR. If the L values averaged at time n are \{x}{
\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 , x}{\cf2\dn n-1}{\cf2 , ... , x}{\cf2\dn n-L+1}{\cf2 \} 
then the present and past values are used and the filter is called 
causal. We will usually make this assumption. But note that if the 
data is in memory- e.g., a digital image- there is no compelling 
reason to assume causality. Notice also that near the ends of the data 
sequence special provision must be made: L data values are not 
available. We could modify the form of the moving average but this 
would destroy its unchanging, or invariant, form, which enables 
powerful Fourier analysis that is central to our discussions. Rather, 
we change the data sequence! For instance, we could supply the missing 
values as zeros.}}
.TXT 34 0 132 0 0
Cg a50.250000,50.250000,387
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 We now 
turn to a more general treatment of FIR filters. Given a finite 
length, digital signal \{x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 , n=0,...,J-1\}, a 
new discrete-time signal \{y}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2\dn ,}{\cf2  n=0,...,N-1\}  
is formed as follows. At each time, n, y}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2  is formed 
as a weighted sum of the values \{x}{\cf2\dn m}{\cf2 , m=n,...,n-M\}, 
the weighting given by a finite-length sequence \{h}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{
\cf2 , n=0,...,M\}.  We begin to implement a simple example of this 
computation.}}
.TXT 16 0 455 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,7
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\b signal:}}
.EQN 0 10 133 0 0
{0:J}NAME:24
.EQN 0 7 135 0 0
{0:j}NAME:0;{0:J}NAME-1
.EQN 0 11 136 0 0
({0:x}NAME)[({0:j}NAME):1
.TXT 5 -28 361 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,8
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\b weights:}}
.EQN 0 10 137 0 0
{0:M}NAME:8
.EQN 0 7 138 0 0
{0:m}NAME:0;{0:M}NAME
.EQN 0 11 139 0 0
({0:h}NAME)[({0:m}NAME):(1)/({0:M}NAME+1)
.TXT 8 -28 363 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,11
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\b new signal:}}
.EQN 0 10 140 0 0
{0:N}NAME:64
.EQN 0 7 141 0 0
{0:n}NAME:0;{0:N}NAME-1
.EQN 0 11 110 0 0
({0:y}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):((0,{0:M}NAME,{0:m}NAME,({0:h}NAME)[({0:m}NAME)*({0:x}NAME)[({0:n}NAME-{0:m}NAME)){64})
.TXT 0 18 3 0 0
Cg a10.000000,10.000000,5
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 (2.1)}}
.TXT 8 -46 4 0 0
Cg a49.250000,49.250000,355
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 In 
Mathcad an equation may be toggled between active and inactive states. 
If we toggle the equation (2.1) to the active state, Mathcad gives us 
an error message "index out of bounds". (Try it! After clicking on 
(2.1), select }{\cf2\b Math}{\cf2  then }{\cf2\b Toggle Equation}{\cf2  
and press F9) This is because x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n-m}{\cf2  is not 
defined for (n-m) not in [0,J-1]: we must supply a definition!}}
.TXT 15 0 5 0 0
Cg a49.000000,49.000000,793
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 Recall 
that in Section 1 we mentioned two  ways to extend the sequence \{x}{
\cf2\dn j}{\cf2 \}. First, we can extend the definition of x by zero 
padding. Second, we can extend the definition of x by periodic 
extension. While the first choice may seem natural and the second 
choice awkward, we will see that the second choice in fact arises as a 
matter of course when we later choose to compute the convolution using 
an alternative algorithm that can require much less computation than 
(2.1). We will thus be concerned with both extensions, resulting in, 
respectively, an aperiodic convolution and a periodic convolution. In 
digital signal processing, we are often concerned with arranging 
matters so that the latter produces the former: this is a task 
requiring some art, on which we will later spend some time. }}
.TXT 30 0 144 0 0
Cg a49.875000,49.875000,493
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 Given 
that we complete the definition of (2.1), it is called a }{\cf2\b 
convolution sum}{\cf2  or, more briefly, a }{\cf2\b convolution}{\cf2 
. In the context of DSP, the convolution defines the operation of a }{
\cf2\b filter}{\cf2  and \{x}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \} and \{y}{\cf2\fs16
\dn n}{\cf2 \} are called, respectively, the }{\cf2\b input sequence}{
\cf2  and the}{\cf2\b  output sequence}{\cf2 . The sequence of weights \{h}{
\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \}, for reasons that we will soon discover, is 
called the }{\cf2\b impulse response (IR)}{\cf2  of the filter. 
Because \{h}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2 \} is of finite length, the filter 
is said to be a }{\cf2\b finite impulse response}{\cf2\b  (FIR)}{\cf2
\b  filter}{\cf2 . }}
.EQN 19 -7 109 0 0
