{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 We see 
that they are the same. This exemplifies the }{\cf2\b time invariance}{
\cf2  property of the filter defined by (2.2). Note that, for finite 
sequences, we had to take care that translated sequences remained 
defined. For the aperiodic convolution we did this by zero padding 
sequences to sufficient length.}}
.TXT 12 0 113 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,379
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset2\fnil Symbol;}}\plain
\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 Given a filter defined by the sequence h}{\cf2
\dn n}{\cf2  and (2.2), let y1}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2  and y2}{\cf2\dn n}{
\cf2  be the outputs corresponding to, respectively, the inputs x1}{
\cf2\dn n}{\cf2  and x2}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 . Then, for any scalars }{\cf2
\f1 a}{\cf2  and }{\cf2\f1 b}{\cf2 , the input x}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 =}{
\cf2\f1 a }{\cf2 x1}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 +}{\cf2\f1 b }{\cf2 x2}{\cf2\dn n}{
\cf2  causes the output y}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 =a y1}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 +}{
\cf2\f1 b }{\cf2 y2}{\cf2\dn n}{\cf2 . We then say that the digital 
filter is }{\cf2\b linear}{\cf2 . This property of the filter accrues 
because it is defined directly as a sum by (2.2) as we easily see.}}
.EQN 17 0 264 0 0
({0:y}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):((0,{0:M}NAME,{0:m}NAME,({0:h}NAME)[({0:m}NAME)*(({0:n}NAME-{0:m}NAMEò0)*({0:n}NAME-{0:m}NAMEó{0:J}NAME-1))*({0:\a}NAME*({0:x1}NAME)[({0:n}NAME-{0:m}NAME)+{0:\b}NAME*({0:x2}NAME)[({0:n}NAME-{0:m}NAME))){64})
.EQN 9 0 268 0 0
