{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\fs36\b Periodic Extension}}
.TXT 4 12 30 0 0
Cg a49.875000,49.875000,358
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 Let n 
be any integer and N a nonnegative integer. Then, for some integer k, 
we can always write n=m+k*N where m is in [0,N-1]. We denote such an m 
by }{\cf2\b pod(n,N)}{\cf2 . Mathcad has a built-in function }{\cf2\b mod(n,N)
}{\cf2  defined as \{remainder of n/N\}: thus mod(n,N) is in 
(-(N-1),N-1) and has the sign of n. We can therefore implement in 
Mathcad our desired function as follows.}}
.EQN 15 0 31 0 0
{0:pod}NAME({0:n}NAME,{0:N}NAME):{0:mod}NAME({0:N}NAME+{0:mod}NAME({0:n}NAME,{0:N}NAME),{0:N}NAME)
.TXT 0 46 32 0 0
Cg a9.000000,9.000000,5
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 (2.3)}}
.TXT 4 -46 34 0 0
Cg a49.000000,49.000000,326
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 To see 
that this is the desired function, note that, first, when n is not 
negative, mod(n,N) is in [0,N-1]. Then adding N and again taking 
mod(.,N) does not change the result. Second, when n is negative, 
mod(n,N) is negative and in [-(N-1),-1]. Then adding N  has a result 
in [1,N-1] which is unchanged when mod(.,N) is taken.}}
.EQN 11 -4 426 0 0
