{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\fs36\b Aperiodic and Periodic Convolutions}}
.TXT 4 12 218 0 0
Cg a48.875000,48.875000,519
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 
Consider the following filtering problem. The filter is an FIR filter 
with impulse response of length M+1 and defined over n=0,...,M. The 
input signal is a sequence of length J and defined over n=0,...,J-1. 
We wish to compute the output signal y}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2  that 
results from the aperiodic convolution (2.2), which we now know to be 
a sequence of length N = M+J and defined over n = 0,...,N-1.  This 
computation explicitly uses the input sequence extended to a length N 
by zero padding and defined to be zero for negative indices.}}
.TXT 20 0 84 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,266
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 We may 
try to compute the sequence y}{\cf2\fs16\dn n}{\cf2  using the 
periodic convolution (2.5). To do this, we, first, extend the input 
sequence to a length N by zero padding and, second, compute the 
periodic convolution (2.5), which further extends the input sequence 
to have period N.}}
.TXT 13 0 222 0 0
Cg a49.125000,49.125000,182
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 From 
Example 2.2 and Exercise 2.8 we see that the aperiodic and periodic 
convolutions will compute the same possibly nonzero values, provided 
that N is greater than or equal to M+J. }}
.TXT 9 0 289 0 0
Cg a50.000000,50.000000,238
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 This 
is an important observation because the periodic convolution can be 
implemented by a fast algorithm as we will discuss. Except in special 
cases, the direct implementation of either convolution is too slow to 
be of practical interest.}}
.TXT 13 -12 224 0 0
Cg a73.000000,73.000000,39
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\fs36\b A Digital Signal Processing Application}
}
.TXT 4 12 86 0 0
Cg a49.375000,49.375000,833
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\fs24\b E}{\cf2\f1\fs24\b xample 2.3}{
\cf2\b  ____________________________________________}{\cf2 \par A data 
sequence is the sum of a slowly varying signal sequence and a rapidly 
varying noise sequence. The noise sequence is modeled by a sequence of 
uncorrelated, zero mean normal random variables. We wish to design a 
data processor that will remove the effects of the noise as much as 
possible. The following  thought leads us to consider a MA, or FIR, 
filter. The MA filter, of length M, averages M contiguous samples of 
the data. If the noise has a     zero mean value sequence, then, if M 
is sufficiently large, an output noise sample   will be, while random, 
quite close to its mean value of zero. The limit on M will be 
determined by the need to have the output signal still follow the form 
of the input signal: that is, the signal sequence should be negligibly 
averaged.}}
.TXT 32 0 227 0 0
Cg a49.250000,49.250000,102
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 We 
consider first the case of a constant signal and leave the case of a 
varying signal to Project 2.1.}}
.TXT 6 0 228 0 0
Cg a50.125000,50.125000,110
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2 The 
data is modeled as a random sequence. Thus we need to be able to 
compute   a sample sequence of the data. }}
.TXT 6 0 89 0 0
Cg a29.625000,29.625000,22
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\b data length and index:}}
.EQN 0 18 87 0 0
{0:J}NAME:512
.EQN 0 12 88 0 0
{0:j}NAME:0;{0:J}NAME-1
.TXT 4 -30 92 0 0
Cg a42.000000,42.000000,16
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green64\blue128;\red0\green0\blue128;}{
\fonttbl{\f0\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}
\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\cf2\f1\b constant signal:}}
.EQN 0 18 230 0 0
